474:
In support and furtherance of its findings and determination that recreation, tourism and commerce are among the city's most valued and important assets and that the promotion of recreation, tourism and commerce is essential to the promotion of health, prosperity, and general welfare of the people;
The city council hereby declares it to be the purpose and intent of this sections 46-4.1 through 46-4.9 to establish an excise tax on rental motor vehicles, the revenue from which, will be designated for the promotion of recreation, tourism and commerce and for the construction or repair of facilities related thereto.
(Ord. No. 0-25-02, art. 1, 6-20-02)

477:
(a) Proceeds derived from the tax levied hereby shall be spent solely for one or more of the following authorized purposes and projects:
(1) The promotion of tourism;
(2) The promotion of commerce;
(3) The construction, maintenance, repair, remodel, or rehabilitation of the Fayetteville Amphitheater;
(4) The construction, maintenance or repair of pedestrian walkways and plazas associated with recreation, commerce or tourism or associated with facilities aimed at the promotion of recreation, commerce or tourism;
(5) Retirement of debt issued to fund the acquisition, construction or renovation of convention, trade, sports, recreational or public safety facilities.
(b) Amounts collected pursuant hereto may be expended pursuant to a contract or contracts with the City of Fayetteville Downtown Development Authority in furtherance of the purposes hereof.
(Ord. No. 0-25-02, art. 1, 6-20-02)

537:
(a) If any holder of a business registration issued by the city should be engaged in unlawful activities, acts which would constitute violations of laws of the State of Georgia, city ordinances, city zoning regulations, city heating, electrical, building codes, and Fayette County health codes, and where such unlawful activities are such as to directly or indirectly affect their qualification to conduct the business for which they were registered or involves the conduct of such business, and where the holder is doing business within the city, the business registration may be revoked or suspended in the manner provided herein.
(b) Where it is reported to the city manager that a holder of a business license registration is engaged in such unlawful activities, a preliminary investigation shall be conducted by the city manager or his designee in order to determine whether there is a basis for the reports. If the city manager's preliminary investigation reveals that there may be a basis for revocation or suspension of the registration, the holder will be notified to appear before the city manager on a date certain and show cause why his business registration should not be revoked or suspended. The holder may appear in person at said hearing or be represented by counsel. At the conclusion of the hearing, the city manager, based upon evidence submitted at said hearing, shall enter an order making a finding of fact and then:
(1) Find that the evidence does not authorize revocation or suspension;
(2) Issue a warning to the holder;
(3) Suspend the registration and probate suspension;
(4) Suspend the registration for up to 30 days;
(5) Suspend the registration pending a hearing by the mayor and city council.
(c) Within ten days from the date of the order from the city manager, the holder may appeal the decision of the city manager by filing a notice of appeal with the city manager. Any decision of the city manager to suspend pending a hearing by the mayor and city council shall automatically be scheduled for a hearing before the city council and mayor no later than 30 days from the date of the suspension. Notice of appeal shall supersede any suspension, pending hearing, except a suspension pending a hearing by the mayor and council on revocation. The city manager shall schedule a hearing before the mayor and city council and notify the holder of the time and date of the hearing. Either party shall have the right to subpoena witnesses at this hearing. At the conclusion of the hearing, the mayor and council may affirm, overrule, or partially affirm and partially overrule the decision of the city manager.
(d) The mayor and city council are authorized to:
(1) Find in favor of the holder;
(2) Issue a warning to the holder;
(3) Suspend the registration and probate suspension;
(4) Revoke the registration and probate revocation;
(5) Revoke the registration; and
(6) Take any other appropriate action regarding holder.
(Ord. No. 0-24-94, § 16-36, 11-21-94)

580:
The city manager is hereby authorized to exercise such powers as may be necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purposes of this article, including the following powers, in addition to others herein granted:
(1) To investigate the dwelling conditions in the city in order to determine which dwellings, buildings, or structures therein are unfit for human habitation or unfit for current commercial, industrial, or business use;
(2) To administer oaths and affirmations, to examine witnesses, and to receive evidence;
(3) To enter upon premises for the purpose of making examinations; provided, however, that such entries shall be made in such a manner as to cause the least possible inconvenience to the persons in possession;
(4) To appoint and fix the duties of such officers, agents, employees of the city as he deems necessary to carry out the purposes of this article; and
(5) To delegate any of his functions and powers under this article to such officers, agents and employees of the city as he may designate.
(Ord. No. 0-30-96, § 15-7, 6-17-96)
